\begin_layout Title
Optic Flow: Forward Model
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The explicit map from body-frame ego-motion 
\begin_inset Formula $(\boldsymbol{\Omega},\mathbf{T})$
\end_inset

 and per-column depth 
\begin_inset Formula $Z_{i}$
\end_inset

 to the two scalar retinal flow measurements 
\begin_inset Formula $(u_{\text{right},i},u_{\text{up},i})$
\end_inset

 at each viewing direction.
 This is the generator inverted by the ego-motion estimators; see optic_flow.lyx
 for the inverse problem.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*
Conventions
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Right-handed body frame: 
\begin_inset Formula $+x$
\end_inset

 forward, 
\begin_inset Formula $+y$
\end_inset

 left, 
\begin_inset Formula $+z$
\end_inset

 up.
 Angular velocity 
\begin_inset Formula $\boldsymbol{\Omega}=(\Omega_{x},\Omega_{y},\Omega_{z})$
\end_inset

 by the right-hand rule: 
\begin_inset Formula $\Omega_{x}$
\end_inset

 = roll, 
\begin_inset Formula $\Omega_{y}$
\end_inset

 = pitch down, 
\begin_inset Formula $\Omega_{z}$
\end_inset

 = yaw left.
 Translational velocity 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{T}=(T_{x},T_{y},T_{z})$
\end_inset

 in the same frame.
 Retinal scalars: 
\begin_inset Formula $u_{\text{right}}>0$
\end_inset

 is rightward image motion, 
\begin_inset Formula $u_{\text{up}}>0$
\end_inset

 upward (arc length per time).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*
Geometry
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
A column views direction 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{d}(\phi,\theta)$
\end_inset

 with 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta$
\end_inset

 the polar angle from 
\begin_inset Formula $+z$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\phi$
\end_inset

 the azimuth from 
\begin_inset Formula $+x$
\end_inset

 toward 
\begin_inset Formula $+y$
\end_inset

:
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\mathbf{d}=\begin{pmatrix}\sin\theta\cos\phi\\
\sin\theta\sin\phi\\
\cos\theta
\end{pmatrix}.
\]

\end_inset

The orthonormal tangent basis at 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{d}$
\end_inset

 is
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\mathbf{e}_{\theta}=\partial_{\theta}\mathbf{d}=\begin{pmatrix}\cos\theta\cos\phi\\
\cos\theta\sin\phi\\
-\sin\theta
\end{pmatrix},\qquad\mathbf{e}_{\phi}=\frac{1}{\sin\theta}\partial_{\phi}\mathbf{d}=\begin{pmatrix}-\sin\phi\\
\cos\phi\\
0
\end{pmatrix}.
\]

\end_inset

with 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{e}_{\theta}$
\end_inset

 pointing down the retina and 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{e}_{\phi}$
\end_inset

 pointing left.
 The triad 
\begin_inset Formula $(\mathbf{d},\mathbf{e}_{\theta},\mathbf{e}_{\phi})$
\end_inset

 is right-handed:
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\mathbf{d}\times\mathbf{e}_{\theta}=\mathbf{e}_{\phi},\qquad\mathbf{d}\times\mathbf{e}_{\phi}=-\mathbf{e}_{\theta},\qquad\mathbf{e}_{\theta}\times\mathbf{e}_{\phi}=\mathbf{d}.
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*
3D flow vector
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The static world counter-moves under self-motion, so the flow of viewing
 direction 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{d}_{i}$
\end_inset

 (a 3D tangent vector, 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{u}_{i}\cdot\mathbf{d}_{i}=0$
\end_inset

) is
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\mathbf{u}_{i}=-\boldsymbol{\Omega}\times\mathbf{d}_{i}-\frac{1}{Z_{i}}\Pi_{i}\mathbf{T},\qquad\Pi_{i}=I-\mathbf{d}_{i}\mathbf{d}_{i}^{\intercal}.
\]

\end_inset

The rotation term 
\begin_inset Formula $-\boldsymbol{\Omega}\times\mathbf{d}_{i}$
\end_inset

 is depth-independent; the translation term projects 
\begin_inset Formula $-\mathbf{T}$
\end_inset

 onto the tangent plane and scales by inverse depth.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*
Projection to retinal scalars
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The 3D vector is assembled from the two retinal scalars as 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{u}_{i}=-u_{\text{right},i}\mathbf{e}_{\phi,i}-u_{\text{up},i}\mathbf{e}_{\theta,i}$
\end_inset

, so by orthonormality
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
u_{\text{right},i}=-\mathbf{u}_{i}\cdot\mathbf{e}_{\phi,i},\qquad u_{\text{up},i}=-\mathbf{u}_{i}\cdot\mathbf{e}_{\theta,i}.
\]

\end_inset

Substituting the forward model and using 
\begin_inset Formula $\Pi_{i}\mathbf{e}_{\phi}=\mathbf{e}_{\phi}$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $\Pi_{i}\mathbf{e}_{\theta}=\mathbf{e}_{\theta}$
\end_inset

 together with 
\begin_inset Formula $(\boldsymbol{\Omega}\times\mathbf{d})\cdot\mathbf{c}=\boldsymbol{\Omega}\cdot(\mathbf{d}\times\mathbf{c})$
\end_inset

 and the triad identities gives the compact forward model:
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\boxed{\;u_{\text{right},i}=-\,\boldsymbol{\Omega}\cdot\mathbf{e}_{\theta,i}+\frac{1}{Z_{i}}\,\mathbf{T}\cdot\mathbf{e}_{\phi,i},\qquad u_{\text{up},i}=\boldsymbol{\Omega}\cdot\mathbf{e}_{\phi,i}+\frac{1}{Z_{i}}\,\mathbf{T}\cdot\mathbf{e}_{\theta,i}\;}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*
Expanded in components
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
With the basis components written out,
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
u_{\text{right},i}=-\big(\Omega_{x}\cos\theta\cos\phi+\Omega_{y}\cos\theta\sin\phi-\Omega_{z}\sin\theta\big)+\frac{1}{Z_{i}}\big(-T_{x}\sin\phi+T_{y}\cos\phi\big),
\]

\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula 
\[
u_{\text{up},i}=\big(-\Omega_{x}\sin\phi+\Omega_{y}\cos\phi\big)+\frac{1}{Z_{i}}\big(T_{x}\cos\theta\cos\phi+T_{y}\cos\theta\sin\phi-T_{z}\sin\theta\big),
\]

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $(\phi,\theta)=(\phi_{i},\theta_{i})$
\end_inset

 are the column's azimuth and polar angle.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*
Linear form
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Both scalars are linear in the 6-vector 
\begin_inset Formula $[\boldsymbol{\Omega};\mathbf{T}]$
\end_inset

, which is what the closed-form known-depth estimator exploits.
 Stacking the two rows per column,
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\begin{pmatrix}u_{\text{right},i}\\
u_{\text{up},i}
\end{pmatrix}=\begin{pmatrix}-\mathbf{e}_{\theta,i}^{\intercal} & \frac{1}{Z_{i}}\mathbf{e}_{\phi,i}^{\intercal}\\
\mathbf{e}_{\phi,i}^{\intercal} & \frac{1}{Z_{i}}\mathbf{e}_{\theta,i}^{\intercal}
\end{pmatrix}\begin{pmatrix}\boldsymbol{\Omega}\\
\mathbf{T}
\end{pmatrix}.
\]

\end_inset

Columns with non-finite depth keep only their rotation block (
\begin_inset Formula $1/Z_{i}\to0$
\end_inset

), the correct limit for infinitely distant points.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*
Two offset eyes
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
An eye at body-frame offset 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{r}_{e}$
\end_inset

 from the center of mass moves at 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{T}_{e}=\mathbf{T}+\boldsymbol{\Omega}\times\mathbf{r}_{e}$
\end_inset

, so its columns use 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{T}_{e}$
\end_inset

 in place of 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{T}$
\end_inset

 above; 
\begin_inset Formula $\boldsymbol{\Omega}$
\end_inset

 is shared.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*
Sanity checks
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
Pure yaw at the equator.
\series default
 With 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta=\pi/2$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\boldsymbol{\Omega}=(0,0,\Omega_{z})$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{T}=0$
\end_inset

: 
\begin_inset Formula $u_{\text{right}}=\Omega_{z}$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $u_{\text{up}}=0$
\end_inset

.
 Yaw-left drives uniform rightward flow, depth-independent.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
Pure forward translation.
\series default
 With 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{T}=(T_{x},0,0)$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $\boldsymbol{\Omega}=0$
\end_inset

: 
\begin_inset Formula $u_{\text{right}}=-\frac{T_{x}}{Z}\sin\phi$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $u_{\text{up}}=\frac{T_{x}}{Z}\cos\theta\cos\phi$
\end_inset

.
 Flow vanishes at the focus of expansion 
\begin_inset Formula $(\phi=0,\theta=\pi/2)$
\end_inset

 and grows with 
\begin_inset Formula $1/Z$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

